<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical} ogType="article" jsonLd={[articleJsonLd]} author="World Battery Hub" dateModified="2026-11-05">
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="byline">Applications · Last reviewed 2026-11-05</p>
      <h1>Battery applications</h1>
      <p class="lead">Battery choice starts with the duty cycle, not the chemistry. This hub groups the application pages by what the battery is asked to do — cranking, cycling, standby or traction — and links the specification that decides each one.</p>

      <div class="takeaways">
        <strong>Quick answer:</strong> Match the battery to the load profile first. <strong>SLI/start-stop</strong> needs high short-duration current; <strong>storage/solar</strong> needs cycle life at partial state of charge; <strong>marine and RV</strong> need dual-purpose behaviour; <strong>telecom/UPS</strong> needs standby life and low self-discharge; <strong>forklift</strong> needs deep daily cycling. Chemistry follows from that profile, not the other way round.
      </div>

      <h2>Application clusters</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Application</th><th>What decides the specification</th><th>Start here</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Automotive SLI &amp; start-stop</td><td>CCA at the installed temperature, reserve capacity, AGM/EFB compatibility</td><td><a href="/lead-acid/sli-battery/">SLI batteries</a> · <a href="/lead-acid/start-stop-battery/">Start-stop</a></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Marine</td><td>Dual-purpose cranking + cycling, vibration, corrosion</td><td><a href="/lead-acid/marine-battery/">Marine batteries</a></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Solar &amp; off-grid storage</td><td>Depth of discharge, cycle life, charge acceptance</td><td><a href="/lead-acid/solar-battery/">Solar batteries</a> · <a href="/lithium/storage/">Lithium storage</a></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Telecom</td><td>Standby life, float voltage, low self-discharge</td><td><a href="/lead-acid/telecom-battery/">Telecom batteries</a></td></tr>
          <tr><td>UPS &amp; data centre</td><td>Short-duration high-rate discharge, impedance, monitoring</td><td><a href="/lead-acid/ups-battery/">UPS batteries</a></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Forklift &amp; traction</td><td>Daily deep cycling, watering/charging infrastructure, counterweight</td><td><a href="/lead-acid/forklift-battery/">Forklift batteries</a></td></tr>
          <tr><td>RV &amp; camper</td><td>Deep-cycle tolerance, weight, charging from alternator/solar</td><td><a href="/lead-acid/rv-battery/">RV batteries</a></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Motorcycle &amp; powersport</td><td>Small format, vibration, cold cranking, maintenance-free</td><td><a href="/lead-acid/motorcycle-battery/">Motorcycle batteries</a></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Truck &amp; commercial</td><td>CCA, vibration, dual-battery systems, long idle loads</td><td><a href="/lead-acid/truck-battery/">Truck batteries</a></td></tr>
          <tr><td>E-bike &amp; light EV</td><td>Energy density, pack voltage, BMS, cycle life</td><td><a href="/lithium/e-bike-battery/">E-bike batteries</a></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>How to choose without over-specifying</h2>
      <p>Write down four numbers before comparing products: the load in watts or amps, the duration, the ambient temperature range, and the acceptable weight. Those four decide whether a lead-acid or lithium product is even in the running. Only then compare CCA, Ah, cycle life and price — otherwise you are comparing catalogues, not fit.</p>
      <p>Use the <a href="/tools/runtime-calculator/">runtime calculator</a> for discharge time, the <a href="/lead-acid/sizing-calculation/">sizing calculation</a> for capacity, and the <a href="/compare/">comparison pages</a> for chemistry trade-offs. For wholesale selection, the <a href="/procurement/">procurement hub</a> covers MOQ, Incoterms and supplier verification.</p>

      <h2>Application is not a chemistry</h2>
      <p>The same application can be served by more than one chemistry. A solar installation may use flooded lead-acid, AGM or LFP depending on budget, cycle requirement and available space. Treat any "best chemistry for X" claim as a starting hypothesis, not a specification; the duty cycle and the total cost of ownership decide.</p>

